===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|handen}}, {{m|enm|honden}}, from the noun (see above); and also from {{m|enm|henden}} (> {{cog|en|hend}}), from {{inh|en|ang|*hendan}}, {{m|ang|ġehendan|t=to seize by hand, grasp, hold}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*handijan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*handijaną|t=to take by hand, grasp}}, from the noun (see above). Cognate with {{cog|ofs|handa}}, {{m|ofs|henda|t=to grasp, seize}}, {{cog|gml|handen}}, {{m|gml|henden}} (in derivatives), {{cog|nl|handen}}, {{m|nl|henden|t=to arrange, dispose, be handy}}, {{cog|nl|overhandigen|t=to hand, hand over}}, {{cog|gmh|handen|t=to cut, hew}}, {{cog|gmh|henden|t=to give hands to; take hold of, seize}}, {{cog|non|henda|t=to grasp, seize, take by hand}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|ditransitive}} To give, pass or transmit with the hand, literally or figuratively.
#: {{ux|en|He '''handed''' them the letter.&nbsp;&nbsp; She '''handed''' me an opportunity.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-08-10|volume=408|issue=8848|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/leaders/21583277-worlds-biggest-polluter-going-green-it-needs-speed-up-transition-can-china Can China clean up fast enough?]
|passage=It has jailed environmental activists and is planning to limit the power of judicial oversight by '''handing''' a state-approved body a monopoly over bringing environmental lawsuits.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023 March 8|author=Paul Salveson|title=Fond farewells to two final trains...|journal=RAIL|issue=978|page=54|text=However, Anyon Kay remembers a Mr Walton Ainsworth, of Beech House, Rivington, who owned mills in Bolton, being a regular user before the First World War. He used to drive by horse and trap from his mansion to catch the 0906 train to Bolton each day. Before arriving at the station, local newsagent Tom Dutton would '''hand''' Mr Ainsworth his morning paper!}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To lead, guide, or assist with the hand; to conduct.
#: {{ux|en|to '''hand''' a lady into a carriage}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To manage.
#* {{RQ:Prior Poems|poem=The Lady's Looking-Glass|page=45|passage=I bleſs my Chain, I '''hand''' my [[oar|Oar]], / Nor think on all I left on Shoar.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To seize; to lay hands on.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|1|1|passage=wee will not '''hand''' a rope more}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|rare}} To pledge by the hand; to handfast.
# {{lb|en|transitive|nautical}} To [[furl]] (a [[sail]]).
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1814|author=John Hamilton Moore|chapter=Examination of a Young Sea Officer|title=The new practical navigator|section=nineteenth edition
|passage=send the people up to '''hand''' the sail, and when up, before they goon the yard, I'll clap the rolling tackle on to  steady it}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1834
|origdate=November 4, 1756
|author=w:Benjamin Franklin
|chapter=Observations in answer to the foregoing.
|title=Memoirs of Benjamin Franklin
|url=https://archive.org/details/membenfrank02frankrich/
|volume=II
|location=[[Philadelphia]]
|publisher=McCarty & Davis
|OCLC=22645948
|page=344
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/membenfrank02frankrich/page/344/
|column=1
|text=In the very long run from the west side of America to Guam, among the Philippine Islands, ships seldom have occasion to '''hand''' their sails, so equal and steady is the gale, and yet they make it in about 60 days, which could not be if the wind blew only in the afternoon.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To [[cooperate]].

=====Usage notes=====
When used in the sense of "to give", "to hand" is unlike many English ditransitives in that specifying the recipient is obligatory, whether as a true argument or within a prepositional phrase ({{w|dative shift}}).

