#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=Emiliano Ippoliti; Thomas Nickles|title=Heuristic Reasoning|url=https://www.scribd.com/doc/290648904/
|passage="The '''heuristic''' appraisal is the 'identification and evaluation of hints and clues that can provide direction to inquiry in the sometimes large gap between the extremes of complete knowledge and complete ignorance'”.}}
# {{lb|en|computing|of a method or algorithm}} That provides a useful, but not [[optimal]], solution to a problem. Such [[algorithm]]s are typically employed either because the only known algorithms that provide optimal solutions use too much [[time]] or [[resources]], or else because there is no known algorithm that provides an optimal solution.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author=Te Chiang Hu; Man-tak Shing|title=Combinatorial Algorithms|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=BF5_bCN72EUC
|passage=If a '''heuristic''' algorithm works for most of the input data or its maximum percentage error is tolerable, we may prefer the '''heuristic''' algorithm to an optimum algorithm that requires a long time.}}
# {{lb|en|of an argument}} That reasons from the value of a method or [[principle]] that has been shown by experimental investigation to be a useful aid in [[learn]]ing, [[discovery]] and [[problem-solving]].

