# {{lb|en|Ancient Greece}} An ancient Greek priest who interpreted [[sacred]] [[mystery|mysteries]], especially the priest of the [[Eleusinian Mysteries]].
#* {{RQ:Bulwer-Lytton Athens|volume=I|page=59|passage=The exhibition of ancient statues, relics, and symbols, concealed from daily adoration (as in the Catholic festivals of this day), probably, made a main duty of the '''Hierophant'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Morley Haunted Bookshop|passage=If books are as provocative as you suggest, one would expect every librarian to utter the shrill screams of a '''hierophant''', to clash ecstatic castanets in his silent alcoves!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Robert Parker|title=Polytheism and Society at Athens|publisher=Oxford University Press|isbn=9780199274833|page=351|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=F_ATDAAAQBAJ&pg=PA351&dq=%22telesterion%22&hl=&cd=24&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22telesterion%22&f=false|text=Most scholars name the rectangular enclosure ''anaktoron'', locate in it the throne of the '''hierophant''', and suppose that from it emanated the central revelation which occurred, in Plutarch's phrase, ‘when the ''anaktora'' were opened’.}}
# An interpreter of sacred mysteries or [[arcane]] knowledge.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1840|passage=Poets are the '''hierophants''' of an unapprehended inspiration; the mirrors of the gigantic shadows which futurity casts upon the present; the words which express what they understand not; the trumpets which sing to battle and feel not what they inspire; the influence which is moved not, but moves. Poets are the unacknowledged legislators of the world.|author=w:Percy Bysshe Shelley|title=w:A Defence of Poetry}} 
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1894|passage=Thus I became the '''hierophant''' of those three worthy and talented men, who, in spite of their literary accomplishments, were not wise, since they were infatuated with occult and fabulous sciences, and believed in the existence of phenomena impossible in the moral as well as in the physical order of things.|author=w:Arthur Machen|title=Memoirs of Jacques Casanova de Seingalt}}
#* {{RQ:Lowry Under the Volcano|page=145|passage=What did even the '''hierophants''' of science know of the potencies of, for them, unvintageable evil?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1975|passage=Ambassadors of northern countries stand / Impassive while our '''hierophants''' intone / Long canticles of Christ the Contraband: / Our grandees' hearts are shrunk to kidney stones.|author=Peter Porter|chapter=Baroque Quatrains Dedicated to James Fenton|title=Living in a Calm Country}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1987|passage=No, I must play creator / And make them up, these '''hierophants'''.|author=Peter Porter|chapter=A Tribute to my enemies|title=The Automatic Oracle}}
# One who [[explain]]s or makes a [[commentary]].

