===Etymology 1===
* The earliest instances are of the latter part of the 17th century.
* Possibly {{back-form|en|hazy|nocap=1}}.
* Compare {{cog|non|höss||grey}}, akin to {{cog|ang|hasu||gray}}. <ref>{{R:Webster 1913|haze}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# Very fine solid particles (smoke, dust) or liquid droplets (moisture) suspended in the air, slightly limiting visibility. {{q|Compare {{m|en|fog}}, {{m|en|mist}}.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1772|month=December|author=James Cook|journal=[[wikisource:A_Voyage_Towards_the_South_pole_and_Around_the_World|A Voyage Towards the South Pole and Around the World]]|volume=1|title=2
|passage=Our hopes, however, soon vanished; for before eight o'clock, the serenity of the sky was changed into a thick '''haze''', accompanied with rain.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1895|author=H.G. Wells|title=s:The Cone|passage=A blue '''haze''', half dust, half mist, touched the long valley with mystery.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-29|volume=407|issue=8842|page=29|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/asia/21580154-forest-fires-bring-record-levels-air-pollution-and-end-not-sight-unspontaneous Unspontaneous combustion]
|passage=Since the mid-1980s, when Indonesia first began to clear its bountiful forests on an industrial scale in favour of lucrative palm-oil plantations, “'''haze'''” has become an almost annual occurrence in South-East Asia.}}
# A reduction of transparency of a clear gas or liquid.
# An analogous dullness on a surface that is ideally highly reflective or transparent.
#: {{ux|en|The soap left a persistent '''haze''' on the drinking glasses.}}
#: {{ux|en|The furniture has a '''haze''', possibly from some kind of wax.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} Any state suggestive of haze in the atmosphere, such as mental confusion or vagueness of memory.
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|XXIV|41|And is it that the '''haze''' of grief<br>⁠Hath stretch’d my former joy so great?<br>⁠The lowness of the present state,<br>That sets the past in this relief?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1957|author=w:Daphne du Maurier|title=[[w:The Scapegoat (novel)|The Scapegoat]]|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=cf4-iVG03pEC|page=218|isbn=081221725X
|passage=In my '''haze''' of alcohol, I thought for one crazy instant that he had plumbed my secret.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=A History of the Concerto|page=312|author=Michael Thomas Roeder|year=1994|passage=But these tasks are difficult for the recent history of the form, since our perceptions are clouded by the '''haze''' of historical proximity.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Salem: Place, Myth, and Memory|page=179|author=Dane Anthony Morrison; Nancy Lusignan Schultz|year=2005|passage=Because he chose to be "a citizen of somewhere else," we glimpse him now only "through the '''haze''' of memory."}}
#* {{quote-av|en|author=w:Eric Pearson; w:Craig Kyle; w:Christopher Yost|director=w:Taika Waititi|actor=w:Tessa Thompson|role=Valkyrie|title={{w|Thor: Ragnarok}}|year=2017|text=I've spent years in a '''haze''', trying to forget my past.  Sakaar seemed like the best place to drink, and to forget... and to die, one day.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|engineering|packaging}} The degree of [[cloudiness]] or [[turbidity]] in a clear glass or plastic, measured in [[percent]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=Leonard I. Nass; Charles A. Heiberger|title=Encyclopedia of PVC|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=mDe7EidmglIC&|page=318|isbn=0824778227
|passage='''Haze''' is listed as a percent value and, typically, is about 1% for meat film.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|brewing}} Any substance causing turbidity in [[beer]] or [[wine]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1985|author=Philip Jackisch|title=Modern Winemaking|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=Zf-24UvvT4oC|page=69|isbn=0801414555
|passage=Various clarifying and fining agents are used in winemaking to remove '''hazes'''.}}

