#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Ulysses S. Grant|chapter=Ancestry—Birth—Boyhood|title=Personal Memoirs of U. S. Grant. In Two Volumes|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Charles L. Webster and Company|Charles L. Webster & Company]]|year=1885|volume=I|page=26|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/personalmemoirsof01gran/page/26/mode/1up|oclc=928835262|passage=When I was seven or eight years of age, I began '''hauling''' all the wood used in the house and shops. I could not load it on the wagons, of course, at the time, but I could drive [the horses], and the choppers would load, and some one at the house unload.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To draw or pull something [[heavy]].
#* {{RQ:Homer Pope et al Odyssey|volume=III|book=XIII|page=194|lines=136–139|passage=Thither they bent, and '''haul'd''' their ſhip to land, / (The crooked keel divides the yellow ſand) / ''[[w:Odysseus|Ulyſſes]]'' ſleeping on his couch they bore, / And gently plac'd him on the rocky ſhore.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:John Denham (poet)|John Denham]]|chapter=The Destruction of Troy. An Essay on the Second Book of {{w|Virgil}}’s [[w:Aeneid|Æneis]]. Written in the Year 1636.|mainauthor=w:Alexander Chalmers|editor=w:Samuel Johnson|title=The Works of the English Poets, from Chaucer to Cowper; {{...|including the Series Edited, with Prefaces, Biographical and Critical, by Dr. Samuel Johnson: And the Most Approved Translations. The Additional Lives by Alexander Chalmers, F.S.A.}} In Twenty-one Volumes|location=London|publisher=Printed &#91;by [[w:Charles Whittingham|C[harles] Whittingham]]&#93; for [[w:Joseph Johnson (publisher)|J[oseph] Johnson]] [''et al.'']|year=1810|volume=VII (Cowley, Denham, Milton)|page=240|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=XUwpAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA240|oclc=277665500|passage=A spacious breach we make, and Troy's proud wall, / Built by the gods, by her own hands doth fall; / Thus all their help to their own ruin give, / Some draw with cords and some the monster drive / With rolls and levers: thus our works it climbs, / Big with our fate; the youth with songs and rhimes, / Some dance, some '''haul''' the rope; at last let down / It enters with a thundering noise the town, / Oh Troy, the seat of gods, in war renown'd!|footer={{small|Earlier editions use the word ''hale'': see ''[[hale#Etymology 3|hale]]''.}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=A. Rogers|chapter=Yachting|title=The Encyclopædia of Sport & Games: In Four Volumes|edition=The Sportsman|location=London|publisher=&#91;[[w:The Sportsman (1865 newspaper)|The Sportsman]]?&#93;|year=1912|volume=IV (Rackets–Zebra)|page=357|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=qV7zAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA357|column=2|oclc=186708828|passage=Passing through the entrance of the harbour, the admiral proceeds to manœuvre his flet, to the great gratification of the host of spectators, {{...}} [H]e hoists Dutch colours and fires two guns. This is the signal for a general chase after an imaginary enemy, a chase which continues till he '''hauls''' down his flag and fires another gun.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1947|month=March and April|title=Notes and News: The Edge Hill Light Railway|journal=Railway Magazine|page=116|text=The cable used for '''hauling''' the wagons on the incline may still be seen, but several of the guiding rollers have disappeared.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=w:Phil McNulty|title=[[w:Crystal Palace F.C.|Crystal Palace]] 1 – 2 [[w:Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]]|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/36292265|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180614123252/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/36292265|archivedate=14 June 2018|work=w:BBC Sport|date=22 May 2016|passage=United lost [[w:Chris Smalling|[Chris] Smalling]] to a second yellow card for '''hauling''' back {{w|Yannick Bolasie}} in extra time&nbsp;– but [[w:Jesse Lingard|[Jesse] Lingard]] took the trophy to Old Trafford when he lashed home a first-time strike from {{w|Damien Delaney}}'s half-clearance after 110 minutes.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[carry]] or [[transport#Verb|transport]] something, with a [[connotation]] that the item is heavy or otherwise [[difficult]] to [[move#Verb|move]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|editor=W. M. Camp|title=Why Not Tell the Truth?|magazine=The Railway and Engineering Review|location=Chicago, Ill.|publisher=Railway Review Inc.{{nb...|Published at 1305 Manhattan Building}}|date=4 February 1905|volume=XLV|issue=5|page=73|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=YhRCAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA73|column=1|oclc=1821156|passage=The California fruit trade is all handled by the Southern Pacific and the Santa Fe railroads. The last named road operates its own refrigerator cars and fixes its own rates. It '''hauls''' fully half of the traffic and it is therefore evident that the "Beef Trust" has no voice or power in the matter. {{...}} The same condition exists with the melon grower of Colorado, except that in this case the Santa Fe road '''hauls''' nearly all of the product.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|figuratively}} To [[drag#Verb|drag]], to pull, to [[tug#Verb|tug]].
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Jonathan Jurejko|title=[[w:Newcastle F.C.|Newcastle]] 3 – 0 [[w:Stoke City F.C.|Stoke]]|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/17711736|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180912130944/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/17711736|archivedate=12 September 2018|work=w:BBC Sport|date=21 April 2012|passage=The 26-year-old &#91;{{w|Papiss Cissé}}&#93; has proved a revelation since his £10m move from [[w:SC Freiburg|Freiburg]], with his 11 goals in 10 matches '''hauling''' Newcastle above [[w:Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Spurs]], who went down to {{w|Adel Taarabt}}'s goal in Saturday's late kick-off at Loftus Road.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|figuratively}} ''Followed by'' '''{{l|en|up}}''': to [[summon]] to be [[discipline#Verb|disciplined]] or [[hold#verb|held]] [[answerable]] for something.
#* {{quote-hansard|en|speaker=w:William Herbert Herries|debate=Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Bill|report=New Zealand. Parliamentary Debates. Fourth Session, Sixteenth Parliament|house=[[w:New Zealand House of Representatives|House of Representatives]]|location=Wellington|publisher=John Mackay, government printer|date=6 August 1908|volume=144|page=188|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=CvELAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA188|column=1|oclc=191255532|passage=Well, it is impossible to carry stock by train or steamer without causing some pain or suffering, and to be '''hauled''' up before a Justice of the Peace {{...}} without a warrant because somebody thinks you have caused unnecessary pain seems to me to be an outrageous proceeding. {{...}} [T]o enact a Draconian law that if anybody who does not know anything about the subject thinks when you are trying to get a bullock into a truck when he will not go you are acting cruelly he can inform the police, and you can be arrested there and then and '''hauled''' up before a Justice of the Peace.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To pull [[apart]], as oxen sometimes do when [[yoked#Adjective|yoked]].
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive|nautical}} To [[steer#Verb|steer]] (a [[vessel]]) [[close#Adjective|closer]] to the [[wind#Noun|wind]].
#: {{antonyms|en|veer}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Charles Fearne|chapter=The Trial at Large of Captain George Burrish,{{nb...|on Board His Majesty’s Ship the London, at Chatham.}}|title=Minutes of the Proceedings of a Court-Martial, Assembled on the 23d of September, 1745, {{...|and Continued to be Held from Time to Time by Adjournment)}} to Enquire into the Conduct of Admiral Matthews [i.e., {{w|Thomas Mathews}}], Vice-Admiral [[w:Richard Lestock|[Richard] Lestock]], and Several Other Officers, in and Relating to the Late Engagement between His Majesty’s Fleet and the Combined Fleets of France and Spain off Toulon|location=London|publisher=Published with His Majesty's royal privilege and licence|date=7 October 1745|year_published=1746|page=240|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Hrjy0DHPPhQC&pg=PA240|oclc=559831649|passage=When the Admiral '''hauls''' out of the Line, and remains ſo for ſome Accident, although the Signal for the Line is flying, and the Signal for Battle then out, ought not the other Ships to continue in the Line, doing their Duty, engaging the Enemy?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:James Cook|editor=[[w:John Hawkesworth (book editor)|John Hawkesworth]]|chapter=[An Account of a Voyage around the World, in the Years MDCCLXVIII, MDCCLXIX, MDCCLXX, and MDCCLXXI. By Lieutenant James Cook, Commander of His Majesty’s Bark the Endeavour.] Chapter VII|title=An Account of the Voyages Undertaken by the Order of His Present Majesty for Making Discoveries in the Southern Hemisphere, {{...|and Successively Performed by Commodore Byron, Captain Wallis, Captain Carteret, and Captain Cook, in the Dolphin, the Swallow, and the Endeavour: Drawn up from the Journals which were Kept by the Several Commanders, and from the Papers of Joseph Banks, Esq; [...]}} In Three Volumes|location=London|publisher=Printed for [[w:William Strahan (publisher)|W[illiam] Strahan]]; and [[w:Thomas Cadell (publisher)|T[homas] Cadell]]{{nb...|in the Strand.}}|date=4 April 1769|year_published=1773|volume=II|page=72|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=zNReAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA72|oclc=745146430|passage=On Tueſday the 4th of April, about ten o'clock in the morning, Mr. Banks's ſervant, Peter Briſcoe, diſcovered land, bearing fourth, at the diſtance of about three or four leagues. I immediately '''hauled''' up for it, and found it to be an iſland of an oval form, with a lagoon in the middle, which occupied much the larger part of it; {{...}}}}
