# An act of [[#Verb|hauling]] or [[pulling#Noun|pulling]], particularly with [[force#Noun|force]]; a ([[violent]]) [[pull#Noun|pull]] or [[tug#Noun|tug]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Sparse Grey Hackle [pseudonym]|chapter=Who is Sparse Grey Hackle?|title=Fishless Days, Angling Nights|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Crown Publishing Group|Crown Publishers]]|year=1971|oclc=164936|title2=Fishless Days, Angling Nights: Classic Stories, Reminiscences, and Lore|location2=New York, N.Y.|publisher2=w:Skyhorse Publishing|year2=2011|page2=22|pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=WTbzme08hJwC&pg=PA22|isbn2=978-1-61608-339-7|passage=So I rigged my big salmon rod, and when I heard him splashing in the shallows across the pool, I put a few Alka Seltzer tablets onto the hook and cast into the darkness. There was a splash and a '''haul''' on my line, and this time I struck with both hands and then began pulling and horsing as hard as I could to bring this big fish over to my side.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Joan Druett|chapter=6|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=sZHWAAAAQBAJ&pg=PT71|title=A Watery Grave|series=Wiki Coffin Adventure|seriesvolume=1|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:St. Martin's Press|St. Martin’s Minotaur]]|year=2004|isbn=978-0-312-33442-0|title2=A Watery Grave|series2=Wiki Coffin Mysteries|seriesvolume2=1|location2=New York, N.Y.|publisher2=[[w:St. Martin's Press|St. Martin’s Press]]|year2=2013|isbn2=978-1-4668-5500-7|passage=Then another series of hollered orders, a heave on the wheel, and the brig did a circuit of the flagship's bow, perilously close to the long bowsprit. A '''haul''' at the braces and back along the larboard side of the ''Vincennes'' the ''Swallow'' ran, losing speed but still sending foam seething along the hull of the sloop of war.}}
# The [[distance#Noun|distance]] over which something is hauled or [[transport#Verb|transported]], especially if [[long#Adjective|long]].
#: {{ux|en|Getting to his place was a real '''haul'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|I find long-'''haul''' travel by airplane tiring.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Victor W[ilfred] Pagé|chapter=Truck Operating Cost Determination|title=The Modern Motor Truck: Design, Construction, Operation, Repair, Commercial Applications [...]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=The Norman W. Henley Publishing Co.{{nb...|2 West 45th Street}}|year=1921|page=893|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=WquCjJ86704C&pg=RA1-PA893|oclc=1709111|passage=The condition, par excellence, in favor of motor truck operation is one involving long '''hauls'''. In fact, it may be almost said that any one having to make long '''hauls''' in his business should motorize at once without further debate, as the case for trucks is practically settled by the mere statement of this condition. {{...}} Transportation involving short '''hauls''' is the obverse of the ideal, and as a general thing represents a condition unfavorable for the operation of motor trucks.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Pat Hanlon|chapter=Scheduling through Hubs|title=Global Airlines: Competition in a Transnational Industry|edition=3rd|location=Oxford, Oxfordshire; Burlington, Mass.|publisher=w:Butterworth-Heinemann|year=2007|section=section 5.6 (Effects on Passengers)|page=232|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=NF1HxR9bLwMC&pg=PA232|isbn=978-0-7506-6439-4|passage=Many routes to/from hubs on which the anti-competitive effects of market power are likely to be most marked are relatively short '''hauls''', whereas many of the through markets most likely to benefit from greater competition are relatively long '''hauls'''. If scheduling through hubs causes fares in through (long '''haul''') markets to fall and fares in local (short '''haul''') markets to rise, this can result in the structure of fares by distance reflecting more closely the manner in which average costs vary by route length.}}
# An [[amount#Noun|amount]] of something that has been [[take#Verb|taken]], especially of [[fish#Noun|fish]], [[illegal#Adjective|illegal]] [[loot#Noun|loot]], or [[item]]s [[purchase#Verb|purchased]] on a [[shop#Verb|shopping]] [[trip#Noun|trip]].
#: {{ux|en|The robber’s '''haul''' was over thirty items.}}
#: {{ux|en|The trawler landed a ten-ton '''haul'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Commissioners of Fisheries. Burlington County.|journal=Seventh Annual Report of the Commissioners of Fisheries of the State of New Jersey, for the Year 1876|location=Trenton, N.J.|publisher=John L. Murphy, State Gazette Printing House|year=1876|page=9|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=rJE9AAAAYAAJ&pg=PA9|oclc=175676180|passage=At Kidney's Cove there was a seine of one hundred and seventy-five fathoms in length and twenty-four feet in depth, operated by a crew of twelve men. The daily '''hauls''' were ten, and was fished from April 13th or June 6th. Gross receipts, $1,600.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Got Away with $25,000 Worth of Tires, It is Said|magazine=[[w:Automotive Industries (magazine)|The Horseless Age: First Automobile Journal in the English Language]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Horseless Age Co.|date=12 April 1911|volume=XXVII|issue=15|page=647|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ovEwAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA647|column=2|oclc=27673485|passage=One of the biggest "'''hauls'''" ever made in the tire business is alleged to have been engineered last week by H. R. Hare, a former cashier of the Hartford Rubber Works. Most of the larger tire concerns doing business in New York were the victims, the total amount stolen being in the neighborhood of $25,000 worth.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Martin Cruz Smith|chapter=|title=[[w:Tatiana (novel)|Tatiana: An Arkady Renko Novel]]|series=Arkady Renko|seriesvolume=8|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Simon & Schuster|year=2013|isbn=978-1-4391-4021-5|edition2=1st trade paperback|location2=New York, N.Y.|publisher2=Simon & Schuster Paperbacks|year2=November 2014|page2=173|pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=9aITAAAAQBAJ&pg=PA173|isbn2=978-1-4391-4021-5|passage=Besides Maxim the only other person in sight was a beachcomber so wrapped in scarves he could have been a pilgrim from the Middle Ages. He dragged a sledge with a '''haul''' of driftwood, bottles and cans.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|work=BBC Sport|date=13 September 2020|author=Andrew Benson|title=Tuscan Grand Prix: Lewis Hamilton claims 90th win after incredible race|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/formula1/54138847|passage=A welcome two-week break after nine races in 11 weeks before the Russian Grand Prix, where Hamilton can equal a record some felt might never be broken - Schumacher's '''haul''' of 91 victories.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260902201017/https://www.bbc.com/sport/formula1/54138847|archivedate=2 September 2026}}
# {{lb|en|Internet}} {{ellipsis of|en|haul video||video posted on the Internet consisting of someone showing and talking about [[recently]] purchased [[item]]s}}.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Rachel Siegel|title=Holiday shopping in the age of Instagram|url=https://www.smh.com.au/business/companies/holiday-shopping-in-the-age-of-instagram-20181222-p50nu9.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20190423124425/https://www.smh.com.au/business/companies/holiday-shopping-in-the-age-of-instagram-20181222-p50nu9.html|archivedate=23 April 2019|newspaper=w:The Sydney Morning Herald|location=Sydney, N.S.W.|publisher=w:Nine Publishing|date=24 December 2018|issn=0312-6315|oclc=958159901|passage=Then there are the YouTube '''hauls''', the oddly viral videos of influencers showing off what they got for Christmas. In her 2017 '''haul''' that's been watched 1.7 million times, Olivia Jade&nbsp;– a 19-year-old beauty and fashion tipster and the daughter of actress {{w|Lori Loughlin}}&nbsp;– sat on a plush white bed in polar bear pyjamas and a Santa hat. One by one, she showed off a bottle of Valentino perfume ("sweet mixed with floral"), a cropped, purple fuzzy sweater from Urban Outfitters, bikinis, sneakers, jeans, underwear, high heels, dresses, tops and more.|accessdate=26 February 2019}}
# {{lb|en|ropemaking}} A [[bundle#Noun|bundle]] of many [[thread#Noun|threads]] to be [[tar#Verb|tarred]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=B. S.|entry={{smallcaps|Rope-making}}|editor=w:Macvey Napier|title=[[w:Encyclopædia Britannica|The Encyclopædia Britannica, Or Dictionary of Arts, Sciences, and General Literature]]|edition=7th|location=Edinburgh|publisher=[[w:A & C Black|Adam and Charles Black]]|year=1842|volume=XIX, issue 2|page=461|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=z1hBAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA461|column=2|oclc=9011603|passage=When the collected yarns number about 400, they are coiled up in a '''haul''', and are ready either for tarring, or laying into white ropes. Previous to the '''haul''' being taken up for tarring, there is a slight turn put into it to keep it from getting entangled in the tar-kettle. {{...}} 400 of such threads constituted a '''haul''', and weighed 12 cwt. 2 qrs, and when tarred 15 cwt.}}
